{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>For from the top of the rocks I see him.</b> His lineage is not from Terach but from Abraham, who was called ״צוּר״ (rock), as it says (Isaiah 51:1–2): ״Look to the rock from which you were hewn… look to Abraham your father.״ And that rock — his work was whole with HaShem, and he burned away all the idols from the earth. ״And from the hills״ — these are the matriarchs; ״I see him״ — from there is the head of his lineage. ״Behold, a people that dwells alone״ and so forth: from the day it was said to Abraham, ״Go for yourself from your land״ and so forth, from then onward he was set apart from every nation, and he has no joining neither to his father's house nor to any nation, for Elohim took him as His portion. And you shall know that this is so, for the Holy One, blessed be He, gave a count to no nation but Israel alone, and this is the thing that indicates their excellence, in the manner we explained above in parashat בְּמִדְבַּר. And of this he said: ״Who has counted the dust of Jacob״ and so forth, for this is a sign that he has no joining with any nation and tongue. And this is what he said: ״and among the nations it is not reckoned,״ for all reckoning (חִשָּׁבוֹן) means number, and he said that all the nations have no particular reckoning but only a general one, whereas Israel have a particular number even when Judah and Israel are as the sand of the sea. And this is what he said: ״Who has counted the dust of Jacob״ — even at a time when they are multitudinous as the dust of the earth, He gave them a particular count. And it was not enough with this number, but even after the number He made them into four banners and gave a second time a count set aside for each banner, which is a quarter of the camp. This is what it says: ״and counted the quarter of Israel״ — and all this, why? But it comes to show that they are the core of the world, as though there were no four directions in the world besides them, and all that is outside them is not reckoned among the four directions of the world. And this is proof that Terach, the father of Abraham, is not included in the settled world, and Abraham does not trace his lineage after him at all."], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {0 "The verse fragment ״וּמִסְפָּר אֶת רֹבַע יִשְׂרָאֵל״ is not a direct quotation of Numbers 23:10 but appears to be the Kli Yakar's compressed paraphrase of the census of the four banners; rendered hyper-literally as it stands."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:42:46.605655896Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}